{
  "paraId": "1578D9FB",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Smoking tobacco in pregnancy is a prevalent, modifiable risk factor for infant mortality and morbidity 1, 2. The global prevalence of smoking during pregnancy was estimated at 1.8%, with Europe reporting a prevalence of 8.1% 74. In the USA, 5.4% of women who gave birth in 2021 reported smoking while being pregnant 3. In 2022, 8.3% of women giving birth in Australia admitted to smoking during the first 20 weeks of pregnancy, with 71% of them continuing to smoke throughout their pregnancy 4. Smoking during pregnancy is associated with an increased risk of several adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes, such as placenta previa, placental abruption, premature rupture of membranes, stillbirth, preterm birth, and small for gestational age 5–9. However, smoking cessation can help reduce these adverse effects 10–12."
}

